<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Adapted from Servo, https://github.com/servo/servo, tag v0.8.0,
  support/macos/Servo.entitlements, MPL-2.0, and Zervo,
  https://github.com/Goddv/Zervo, commit 86cb7126,
  assets/macos/entitlements.plist, MPL-2.0 (the same two keys).

  Entitlements for Mercury Browser's hardened-runtime signature
  (scripts/macos/bundle.sh signs the executable with options=runtime).

  The hardened runtime refuses to map memory that is both writable and
  executable. SpiderMonkey, the JavaScript engine inside Servo, generates
  machine code at run time and needs exactly that:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      lets the process map memory with MAP_JIT, the writable-and-executable
      memory a JIT compiler writes its generated code into.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      lets the process make memory executable that no code signature covers,
      which is what SpiderMonkey's executable-memory allocator asks for when it
      changes page protections around generated code.

  Servo ships exactly these two for the same reason. Measured: the bundle
  signed with the hardened runtime but without them cannot start its
  JavaScript engine (servo-script panics with InitFailed) and loads no page.

  Deliberately absent: com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation (the
  libraries in Contents/MacOS/lib are signed together with the executable) and
  every App Sandbox key (Mercury Browser is not sandboxed). Camera, microphone
  and location would need Info.plist usage strings, not entitlements, and those
  features are off.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
